  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nTuesday through Wednesday, upper wave moves into the north-central \nCONUS, digging southeast towards the Upper Midwest. This will help \npush the aforementioned ridge east of the area, inducing llvl \nsouthwesterly flow over the area through this timeframe. This will \nhelp increase temperatures, especially on Tuesday, along with \nmoisture. A weak bout of energy ahead of the the main shortwave will \npass through Tuesday afternoon and into the night. Moisture and \nforcing will be marginal at that time, limiting precipitation until \nlater in the evening and Tuesday night. Best moisture will be in our \nnorthwest near the approaching surface boundary, where we will start \nto see showers/storms move into the area. Best chances for \nprecipitation will be after midnight Tuesday and through Wednesday, \nwhen the stronger shortwave will move through the area with the slow \nmoving boundary passing through. Thus, surface and upper level \nforcing will favor showers/storms Wednesday, especially for areas \nalong/south of Interstate 80. Much uncertainty exists regarding \nmoisture availability and timing of frontal passage. Thus, this will \nbe something to keep an eye on for updates over the coming days. \nOverall, the severe threat seems low/limited. Best chances for any \nstronger storms will be Wednesday afternoon along/south of \nInterstate 80, where we will be able to build some instability. \nOverall instability/shear balance seems meager at this time though. \n\nAfter the midweek system passes, we will remain on the southern end \nof a broad upper trof over the central CONUS, placing us under the \npath of shortwaves passing along the flow. Thus, we will continue to \nsee near-daily Slight-Chance PoPs late week through the weekend, \nespecially with a baroclinic zone remaining draped over the region. \nAt this time, there seems to be plenty of dry time between waves. \nThus, a complete washout is not expected at this time. Guidance \nfavors stronger southwest flow in the llvls under this pattern, \nwhich will allow for temperatures and humidity to increase during \nthis timeframe.",
